  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2002-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(25 = 5^2\\) and \\(64 = 2^6,\\) we have \\(25^{64}\\cdot 64^{25} = 5^{128}\\cdot 2^{150},\\) so \\[N = \\sqrt{5^{128}\\cdot 2^{150}} = 5^{64}\\cdot 2^{75}.\\]\n\nWriting \\(2^{75} = 2^{64}\\cdot 2^{11},\\) we get \\(N = (5\\cdot 2)^{64}\\cdot 2^{11} = 10^{64}\\cdot 2048.\\)\n\nSo \\(N\\) is \\(2048\\) followed by \\(64\\) zeros, and its digit sum is \\(2 + 0 + 4 + 8 = 14.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Take the square root by writing everything as powers of \\(2\\) and \\(5\\)",
        "\\(N = 5^{64}\\cdot 2^{75} = 10^{64}\\cdot 2^{11}\\); only the digits of \\(2^{11}\\) matter"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
